{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Research an exact-output optimization of the existing Heap permutation traversal. Maintain the number of mismatched adjacency pairs as each Heap swap changes the mapping, updating only affected pairs instead of rescanning the full matrix after every permutation. Initialize the count correctly and include diagonal entries. Preserve the traversal order and the source’s emitted output, including all isomorphisms, so correctness does not depend on the checker accepting a different correspondence.\",\n  \"B\": \"Research inexpensive, correctness-safe rejection filters before permutation search: compare vertex counts, effective adjacency-matrix counts, row-sum multisets, and loop counts; then assess component-size or iterative color-refinement signatures for additional rejection power. Compute these from the boolean adjacency matrices, since duplicate input edges collapse in the source. Never reject based on a differing invariant only when it is genuinely preserved by vertex relabeling; retain the existing output behavior for survivors and measure whether each filter pays for its overhead.\",\n  \"C\": \"Explore a complete constrained isomorphism decision search: refine candidate classes, choose an unmapped vertex with few candidates, and prune assignments that conflict with already-mapped adjacency or diagonal entries. Exhaust the search before declaring non-isomorphism. Test it as a no-isomorphism precheck followed by the original output enumeration for positive cases, which avoids changing source output semantics but may add overhead on isomorphic inputs. A replacement that emits just one mapping is an option only if the official correspondence contract and correctness checker accept it; validate every emitted mapping.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate compatible candidates from A, B, and C without assuming their gains add. Keep the official correctness and timing setup, target, compiler, and budgets unchanged. Preserve accepted output semantics, check correctness across the supplied cases, and compare the integrated run’s measured mean against the valid baseline (0.0083285 s, score 0.5). Retain an integration only when its actual measured result supports it; extra search, refinement, or parallel overhead may make a combined version slower.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The automatic baseline is valid on 4 cases, with three measured times of 0.0082301, 0.0085026, and 0.0082529 seconds (mean 0.0083285 s; score 0.5). The target is ARM64 with 4 cores. The source enumerates permutations and performs up to quadratic adjacency checks per permutation, making redundant validation and non-isomorphism search plausible targets, but the small measured runtime means overhead matters. It also prints every isomorphic permutation, unlike the statement’s description of a correspondence; do not assume a different output will pass an exact-reference check. No optimization measurements are supplied.\"\n}"}}
